You can use an allowlist entry in Lavamoat to permit a specific package to run lifecycle scripts. The example shows enabling scripts for a single package by adding an allowScripts entry for that package in package.json:

"lavamoat": {
  "allowScripts": {
    "sharp": true
  }
}

This demonstrates how to create an allowlist that authorizes lifecycle scripts for the specified package while keep­ing ignore-scripts=true as the default safeguard [1].

Define a custom rate limiter and apply it to your routes.

1) Create the custom limiter in RouteServiceProvider:
RateLimiter::for('custom-limit', function ($request) {
    return Limit::perMinute(5)->by($request->user()?->id ?: $request->ip());
}); [1]

2) Apply the custom limiter to your routes:
Route::middleware('throttle:custom-limit')->get('/dashboard', fn () => 'Dashboard'); [1]

If you also want a global default for other routes, you can combine this with per-route or group throttling as needed, but the above shows how to achieve 5 requests per minute keyed by the logged-in user ID with fallback to IP. [3][4]
